BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Future of AI in Entertainment with James Rowan
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260625T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260625T200000
UID:69d6d173491b809c6f26206d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Generative AI is rapidly reshaping how creative work is imagin
 ed\, produced\, and shared. From image and video generation to writing\, d
 esign\, and virtual production\, these tools are expanding what's possible
  while also raising important questions about authorship\, labour\, and cr
 eative identity.\nIn this talk\, James Rowan explores the current landscap
 e of AI tools in the creative industries\, focusing on how artists\, desig
 ners\, and media practitioners are beginning to integrate them into their 
 workflows. This session will provide a practical overview of key technolog
 ies\, real-world use cases\, and emerging trends\, while also addressing t
 he opportunities and challenges that come with adopting these tools.\nDesi
 gned for a broad audience\, this talk offers a grounded introduction to ge
 nerative AI - what it is\, how it works\, and what it means for the future
  of creative practice.\nDrop in\, no registration required.\nThis program 
 is part of the Innovator in Residence (AI and Music Production) program\, 
 which is designed to help beginner and intermediate learners build knowled
 ge and creative skills and get hands-on with current AI tools in music pro
 duction and learn about the history\, copyright and ethics of generative m
 usic. For more information and a full list of programs\, visit\ntpl.ca/air
 esident\n.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Room 201\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69d6d173491b809c6f26206d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Youth Hub: Rolling in the Hub with Dungeons and Dragons
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260529T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260529T170000
UID:69f26d87c1cf6f9a7588ba22@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Fight fearsome goblins\, discover ancient treasures and explor
 e a fantasy world with your friends in the Youth Hub! Join us for a fun an
 d collaborative storytelling experience led by a youth hub librarian as th
 e game master.\nRegistration for this program is required as space is limi
 ted.\nFor teens only.\nNo prior experience is required and new players are
  welcome to play. Dice and pre-generated character sheets will be provided
  for all participants.\nThis program is part of Youth Hub service. Youth H
 ubs are a welcoming place for teens after school and in the summer\, where
  helpful staff are always available. Free\, nutritious snacks are offered
  daily. Drop by a branch with a\nYouth Hub\nor call the branch to find ou
 t more.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this 
 form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries
  Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be us
 ed for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions
  about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerl
 ine staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto
  ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Northern District\, Youth Hub (on the second floor)
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f26d87c1cf6f9a7588ba22
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Create Hip-Hop Music with AI Tools for Teens
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260514T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260514T170000
UID:69a2019f8089f23c675eb003@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Have you ever had a melody stuck in your head and wished you c
 ould turn it into a real song? AI tools are making it easier than ever to 
 bring your musical experiences to life - but how do you make sure the musi
 c you create is truly yours?\nExplore how to use AI as a creative assistan
 t to build radio-ready hip-hop tracks based on your own original ideas. Yo
 u'll learn how to guide AI tools to serve your vision rather than replace 
 it\, and we'll cover Canadian copyright guidelines to ensure the music you
  make qualifies as your own intellectual property. No prior music or progr
 amming experience is required.\nThis program is part of the Innovator in R
 esidence (AI and Music Production) program\, which is designed to help beg
 inner and intermediate learners build knowledge and creative skills and ge
 t hands-on with current AI tools in music production and learn about the h
 istory\, copyright and ethics of generative music. For more information an
 d a full list of programs\, visit\ntpl.ca/airesident\n.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Youth Hub\, 3rd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a2019f8089f23c675eb003
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:History of Generative Music: The Last 50 Years
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260516T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260516T160000
UID:69a201c84dacf581ff7e6743@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:From the first computer-generated compositions to the AI music
  tools available today\, the past five decades have seen generative music 
 evolve at an extraordinary pace. What began as experiments in university l
 abs has become technology that anyone can access from a laptop or phone.\n
 In this session\, you'll trace the rapid evolution of generative music fro
 m early computer experiments through to modern AI platforms\, discover the
  key innovations that made today's tools possible\, and explore where this
  technology might be heading next.\nDrop-in. No registration required.\nTh
 is program is part of the Innovator in Residence (AI and Music Production)
  program\, which is designed to help beginner and intermediate learners bu
 ild knowledge and creative skills and get hands-on with current AI tools i
 n music production and learn about the history\, copyright and ethics of g
 enerative music. For more information and a full list of programs\, visit\
 ntpl.ca/airesident\n.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Room 201\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a201c84dacf581ff7e6743
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Create Pop Music with AI Tools for Teens
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260613T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260613T160000
UID:69a2037a1e64afd01e5e90ed@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Have you ever had a melody stuck in your head and wished you c
 ould turn it into a real song? AI tools are making it easier than ever to 
 bring your musical experiences to life - but how do you make sure the musi
 c you create is truly yours?\nExplore how to use AI as a creative assistan
 t to build radio-ready pop tracks based on your own original ideas. You'll
  learn how to guide AI tools to serve your vision rather than replace it\,
  and we'll cover Canadian copyright guidelines to ensure the music you mak
 e qualifies as your own intellectual property. No prior musical experience
  is required.\nThis program is for teens. Registration is required.\nThis
  program is part of the Innovator in Residence (AI and Music Production) p
 rogram\, which is designed to help beginner and intermediate learners buil
 d knowledge and creative skills and get hands-on with current AI tools in 
 music production and learn about the history\, copyright and ethics of gen
 erative music. For more information and a full list of programs\, visit\nt
 pl.ca/airesident\n.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Digital Innovation Hub\, 3rd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a2037a1e64afd01e5e90ed
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Music Copyright and Ethics in the AI Era
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260615T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260615T200000
UID:69a203994dacf581ff7e67a1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:When you use AI to create music\, who actually owns it? Can a 
 melody generated by a machine infringe on someone else's copyright? As AI 
 tools become more accessible\, these questions are more relevant than ever
  - and the answers aren't always straightforward.\nIn this session\, you'l
 l get a plain-language breakdown of how music copyright works\, what the r
 ules are around sampling\, and how these long-standing principles apply to
  AI-generated music. You'll also hear about landmark legal cases - both su
 rprising victories and cautionary tales - including real-world examples fr
 om a working composer's own career.\nDrop-in\, no registration required.\n
 This program is part of the Innovator in Residence (AI and Music Productio
 n) program\, which is designed to help beginner and intermediate learners 
 build knowledge and creative skills and get hands-on with current AI tools
  in music production and learn about the history\, copyright and ethics of
  generative music. For more information and a full list of programs\, visi
 t tpl.ca/airesident.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Room 201\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a203994dacf581ff7e67a1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
